  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218944/assets/869e65cb2894403a5fe9f5e5f68e1c7882f8c86aff7596e557958e1f6cbbe348.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(3x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
  "worked_solution": "To graph the function $y=f(3x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218944/assets/b84a3ca0b17949c07748e73235d026d696c199136e448b32639959036c5389a8.png)",
